            <div xml:id="d742">

                <pb facs="0682" n="676" xml:id="pg676"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">481.11/137: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-06-13T15:00:00+01:00">June 13, 1939—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 13—1:06 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1117. Department’s No. 5, June 7, 6 p.m., to Tangier. In the event the Resident
                    General has not yet replied to Blake’s note of June 2 (see Blake’s despatch No.
                    1458, June 2 [<hi rendition="#italic">3</hi>]<anchor n="58" xml:id="d742-f1"/>),
                    would the Department have any objection to the Embassy revealing informally to
                    the Foreign Office the tenor of the telegram referred to above? This suggestion
                    is made in view of the possibility that the Resident General may not fully
                    understand that Coursier’s explanation has made further discussion of the
                    respective positions of the two Governments unnecessary and that the way is
                    therefore clear for immediate examination of the claims.</p>
                <p>Repeated by telegraph to Tangier.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
